{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming 0f65d0e4. I verified the defect at current main c086ee1e rather than the a7a9abe7 the request names, and it is unchanged: internal/workroom/fold.go:1665 rescans every prior artifact at a path to count live predecessors for each new one, so a single-path history is quadratic in the number of artifacts at that path.\n\nThe fix looks small and exact, and the reason it is safe is worth stating before I start. project() runs after the whole log is folded, and retired() reads f.retirementCauses, which is final by then. So a predecessor's retirement status does not change during the projection pass, and a per-path running count of live artifacts computes the identical number that the rescan computes. This is not the shortcut the comment above seenByPath warns about: that one tracked only the immediate predecessor, which let a retirement hide a live ancestor. A count keeps every ancestor.\n\nI will keep seenByPath, because the OmittedSupersessions backward walk still needs the ordered list and is already linear in total artifacts. LivePredecessors, SuccessionUnrecorded, OmittedSupersessions, retirement and succeeded semantics and projection meaning stay as they are.\n\nEvidence I will produce: a large same-path regression with a near-linear gate, correctness cases over interleaved retirement patterns, and mutation proof that the new accounting is actually held by tests rather than merely present. I will branch from current main, rest the implementing commit on this request, publish an exact-head internal/workroom artifact and request independent review.","body":{"branch":"request/linear-artifact-succession"}}